alcemia favor malignancy over chronic infection", "Metabolic Syndrome: Hypercalcemia may indicate hyperparathyroidism, but elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase and extreme leukocytosis are more characteristic of leukemoid reaction than isolated endocrine pathology", "Diabetic Complications: History of diabetes and foot fracture suggest peripheral neuropathy or vascular issues, but systemic findings (leukocytosis, hypercalcemia) and constitutional symptoms dominate the clinical picture"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Markedly elevated leukocyte count", "Hypercalcemia", "Systemic symptoms (fatigue, fever) with no localized organic findings"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.025, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The lack of blasts in peripheral blood and absence of marrow infiltration signs provide moderate exclusion for myeloid neoplasms with increased blasts."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.6181262500162018, "posterior": 0.4280900033165913, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Subacute systemic symptoms and normal lymphocyte differential favor CML. This weakens reactive leukocytosis (leukemoid reaction)."], "evidence_against": ["Hypercalcemia is atypical for CML but could suggest lymphoma/myeloma. Provides moderate support for lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3", "B3.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "urgency", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.146, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Hypercalcemia alone without monoclonal gammopathy weakens multiple myeloma. This provides weak exclusion of plasma cell disorders."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.034, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of infection/inflammation markers and elevated ALP strongly argue against leukemoid reaction. This provides moderate exclusion."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.014, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.26026368421734813, "posterior": 0.26802156729386584, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The subacute systemic symptoms and normal lymphocyte differential are more characteristic of CML than myelodysplastic syndrome, providing moderate evidence for…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Polycythemia Vera (PV)", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1626648026358426, "posterior": 0.09306304419925898, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Normal hemoglobin and erythrocyte morphology provide weak evidence against PV, though abdominal pain remains a potential nonspecific finding."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3.3": {"id": "B3.3", "label": "Essential Thrombocythemia (ET)", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.13013184210867407, "posterior": 0.03722521767970359, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["The normal platelet count and absence of thrombosis provide moderate exclusion for ET, a disorder typically associated with thrombocytosis."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.45, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3.4": {"id": "B3.4", "label": "Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.03, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of marrow fibrosis signs and normal physical exam provide weak exclusion for PMF, which typically presents with splenomegaly and bone pain."]}}, "frontier": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3", "B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute clinical course with constitutional symptoms and normal lymphocyte differential provide stronger support for CML than for myelodysplastic syndrome?", "summary": "The subacute systemic symptoms and normal lymphocyte differential are more characteristic of CML than myelodysplastic syndrome, providing moderate evidence for CML."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of blasts in the peripheral blood differential and lack of marrow infiltration signs (e.g., bone pain, organomegaly) rule out a myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts?", "summary": "The lack of blasts in peripheral blood and absence of marrow infiltration signs provide moderate exclusion for myeloid neoplasms with increased blasts."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of hypercalcemia (Ca²⁺: 10.9) without monoclonal gammopathy or bone marrow fibrosis contradict the diagnosis of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia is atypical for CML but does not definitively exclude it. This finding weakly supports lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of erythrocytosis (normal hemoglobin/hematocrit) and lack of abnormal erythrocyte morphology disfavor polycythemia vera (PV) despite the presence of abdominal pain?", "summary": "Normal hemoglobin and erythrocyte morphology provide weak evidence against PV, though abdominal pain remains a potential nonspecific finding."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the platelet count of 177,000/mm³ and absence of thrombosis/thrombocytosis provide moderate evidence against essential thrombocythemia (ET)?", "summary": "The normal platelet count and absence of thrombosis provide moderate exclusion for ET, a disorder typically associated with thrombocytosis."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of bone marrow fibrosis signs (e.g., splenomegaly, bone pain) and normal physical exam provide moderate exclusion for primary myelofibrosis (PMF)?", "summary": "Lack of marrow fibrosis signs and normal physical exam provide weak exclusion for PMF, which typically presents with splenomegaly and bone pain."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.393], ["B3.1", 0.262], ["B1", 0.197]]}, {"top": [["B3", 0.618], ["B3.1", 0.26], ["B4", 0.213]]}, {"top": [["B3", 0.428], ["B3.1", 0.268], ["B4", 0.146]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch meets posterior threshold for confirmation (0.75). Chronic myeloid leukemia (B3.1) is the leading hypothesis (posterior 0.268) but remains below threshold. Dangerous alternatives like plasma cell disorders (B4, posterior 0.146 ≥ 0.10) remain unresolved. Available discriminators (peripheral blood smear, bone marrow biopsy, monoclonal protein testing) could further refine the differential."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.26802156729386584, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'leukocytosis', 'infection', 'inflammation', 'Myelofibrosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.393}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B3.1", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)", "posterior": 0.268, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B4", "B3.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=86%, source=both]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Polycythemia Vera (PV):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, burning sensation in extremities\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Essential Thrombocythemia (ET):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Polycythemia Vera (PV):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Essential Thrombocythemia (ET):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, ascites\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, ascites\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Polycythemia Vera (PV):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, burning sensation in extremities, confusion\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Essential Thrombocythemia (ET):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF):\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, bleeding easily, blurred vision\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, anorexia\n\nPolycythemia Vera (PV) vs Essential Thrombocythemia (ET):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax"}
